    <DataNode elementId="AT1G43620-MONOMER" textLabel="Sterol 3-beta-glucosyltransferase UGT80B1" type="Protein">
      <Xref identifier="AT1G43620-MONOMER" dataSource="plantcyc" />
      <Graphics centerX="150" centerY="215" width="90.0" height="25.0" textColor="000000" fontName="Arial" fontWeight="Normal" fontStyle="Normal" fontDecoration="Normal" fontStrikethru="Normal" fontSize="12" hAlign="Center" vAlign="Middle" borderColor="000000" borderStyle="Solid" borderWidth="1.0" fillColor="FFFFFF" shapeType="Rectangle" />
      <Comment source="pmngroup">UGT80B1 can be classified as a UDP-glucose:sterol-glucosyltransferase based on the evidence that ugt80b1 mutant plants have reduced steryl glycoside and acyl steryl glycoside levels. Steryl glycosides are commonly found in plant membranes but their biological functions remain poorly understood |CITS: [19641030]| &lt;i&gt;ugt80b1&lt;/i&gt; mutants also have reduced seed size, and a transparent testa phenotype|CITS: [19641030][10384728]|. The mutant seeds also have reduced levels of suberization and lipid polyester monomers. Consistent with this finding, UGT80B1 appears to be expressed in seed coat, as well as other tissues |CITS: [19641030]|. |FRAME: AT3G07020| is a closely related 3&amp;beta;-hydroxy sterol glucosyltransferase. &lt;i&gt;ugt80a2/b1&lt;/i&gt; double mutants have normal levels of celluose suggesting that steryl glycosides are not required to prime cellulose synthesis. In addition, these double mutants are not more sensitive to cold stress than wild type plants as measured by ion leakage and survival at 4&amp;deg; C. This suggests that reduced steryl glycoside and acyl steryl glycoside levels do not impact Arabidopsis cold acclimation |CITS: [19641030]|.</Comment>
      <Property key="UniqueID" value="AT1G43620-MONOMER" />
      <Property key="Synonym_1" value="TRANSPARENT TESTA 15" />
      <Property key="Synonym_2" value="UDP-glucose:sterol glucosyltransferase 80B1" />
      <Property key="Catalyzes" value="RXN-T9-185" />
      <Property key="GOTerms" value="GO:0005774" />
      <Property key="Accession1" value="AT1G43620.1" />
      <Property key="FunctionalAssignmentStatus" value=":PROBABLE-ENZYME" />
      <Property key="Gene" value="AT1G43620" />
      <Property key="^citations" value="17151019" />
      <Property key="InstanceNameTemplate" value="MONOMER-*" />
      <Property key="Locations" value="CCO-VAC-MEM" />
      <CitationRef elementRef="citation_PUB_19641030" />
    </DataNode>
    <DataNode elementId="AT3G07020-MONOMER" textLabel="Sterol 3-beta-glucosyltransferase UGT80A2" type="Protein">
      <Xref identifier="Q9M8Z7" dataSource="uniprot" />
      <Graphics centerX="300" centerY="215" width="90.0" height="25.0" textColor="000000" fontName="Arial" fontWeight="Normal" fontStyle="Normal" fontDecoration="Normal" fontStrikethru="Normal" fontSize="12" hAlign="Center" vAlign="Middle" borderColor="000000" borderStyle="Solid" borderWidth="1.0" fillColor="FFFFFF" shapeType="Rectangle" />
      <Comment source="THE-ARABIDOPSIS-INFORMATION-RESOURCE, dreher">|FRAME: AT3G07020-MONOMER UGT80A2| can be classified as a UDP-glucose:sterol-glucosyltransferase based on direct &lt;i&gt;in vitro&lt;/i&gt; assays |CITS: [9349281][17324374]| as well as evidence that ugt80a2 mutant plants have reduced steryl glycoside and acyl steryl glycoside levels. Steryl glycosides are commonly found in plant membranes but their biological functions remain poorly understood |CITS: [19641030][PATTERSON93]|. |FRAME: AT3G07020-MONOMER UGT80A2| accepts a broad range of sterol substrates and showed the highest activity with transandrosterone. But, it also acts with |FRAME: 3-BETA-HYDROXYANDROST-5-EN-17-ONE 3-&amp;beta;-hydroxyandrost-5-en-17-one|, |FRAME: PREGNENOLONE pregnenolone|, |FRAME: CPD-4143 sitosterol|, |FRAME: CPD-4162 stigmasterol|, deactyl 16-DPA, , 3-&amp;beta;-16,17-&amp;alpha;-epoxypregnenolene, |FRAME:CPD-4161 brassicasterol|, and |FRAME: ERGOSTEROL ergosterol|. Unlike the related |FRAME: MONOMER-16204 SGTL1| from |FRAME: TAX-126910 &lt;i&gt;Withania somnifera&lt;/i&gt;|, |FRAME: AT3G07020-MONOMER UGT80A2| also acts on |FRAME: CHOLESTEROL cholesterol| and 5-&amp;alpha;-cholestan-3&amp;beta;-ol. In addition, |FRAME: AT3G07020-MONOMER UGT80A2| did not accept any sugar donors other than |FRAME: CPD-12575 UDP-D-glucose|. &lt;i&gt;ugt80a2&lt;/i&gt; mutants have reduced seed size |CITS: [19641030]| indicating that steryl glucosides are required for optimal growth and development, but their growth is not severely compromised. There is a related 3&amp;beta;-hydroxy sterol glucosyltransferase in Arabidopsis (UGT80B1 (AT1G43620)) that may compensate for the loss of |FRAME: AT3G07020-MONOMER UGT80A2|. Experiments show that &lt;i&gt;ugt80a2/b1&lt;/i&gt; double mutants have normal levels of celluose suggesting that steryl glycosides are not required to prime cellulose synthesis. In addition, these double mutants are not more sensitive to cold stress than wild type plants as measured by ion leakage and survival at 4&amp;deg; C. This suggests that reduced steryl glycoside and acyl steryl glycoside levels do not impact Arabidopsis cold acclimation |CITS: [19641030]|.</Comment>
      <Property key="UniqueID" value="AT3G07020-MONOMER" />
      <Property key="AlternativeId_Araport" value="AT3G07020.2" />
      <Property key="AlternativeId_Plantcyc" value="AT3G07020-MONOMER" />
